\paragraph{Probe robustness confirms the pattern.}
Cross-instruction probes corroborate: VLA-trained achieves $99.6\%$ at L1, saturating to $100\%$ through L31; Llama-2 base starts at $80.2\%$, saturating by L5 ($100\%$ through L31); untrained decays from $100\%$ (L1) to $82\%$ (L31).
Deep-layer probe stability in both pretrained conditions confirms that signal maintenance derives from structured weights, while the untrained decay reflects absence of organized routing.

\paragraph{Asymmetric training effects.}
Training effects concentrate at the readout token: VLA fine-tuning reshapes instruction attention ($+21$pp over Llama-2 base, $+46$pp over untrained) and improves readout $R^2$ by $+0.069$, while image\_mean metrics change minimally ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:training_effect_summary}).

