\paragraph{Synthesis.}
Our experiments reveal a coherent picture: image tokens remain purely visual across all 32 layers, while the readout token is the sole multimodal convergence point.
VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction routing: language pretraining concentrates instruction attention at mid-depth (L9--L16), while VLA training shifts this to an entry-exit pattern (L0--L1 and L31), enabling immediate instruction access ($99.6\%$ vs.\ pretrained $80.2\%$); untrained routing never exceeds $10\%$.
Training effects concentrate at the readout token ($R^2$ $+0.069$) rather than image representations ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$), and the LLM transforms vision encoder geometry non-monotonically (L8--L13 most action-relevant).

\paragraph{Implications for VLA design.}
Vision encoder pretraining quality is the primary design lever; stronger self-supervised encoders may yield larger gains than scaling the LLM.
Since instructions route solely through the readout token ($|\Delta| < 0.006$; \cite{grant2026}), architectures should optimize routing to this position; L8--L13 appears most action-relevant, though validating specific changes would require policy rollout experiments.

\subsection{Limitations}
We study a single model family (OpenVLA-7B) on LIBERO tabletop tasks; generalization to other architectures (e.g., RT-2~\cite{brohan2023_rt2}, $\pi_0$~\cite{black2024_pi0}), locomotion, or dexterous manipulation remains open.
MLP probes may underestimate representational capacity; RSA measures geometric alignment but not causal contribution; and our post-hoc analyses do not address whether architectural interventions would preserve action quality in closed-loop evaluation.

